        textProvided: "It was in autumn<br><br>[I have before me one of Lewis’s notebooks containing a fragment of a story about a boy who, in his garden, has been having an imaginary joust in the]<br><br>unchartered forests of Logres.<br><br>[...The joust ended, the boy is returning home:]<br><br>To remember suddenly that this was all a game was like hearing the voice of a friend: all the details of that “real” world that lay behind the game–the holidays just begun, the lighted, carpeted rooms, and, presently, the sound of teacups. . . . It was the strangest systole and diastole–no sooner was home regained, than that other world of desert hills and distant, ominous castles enisled in haunted woods, rose up, clothed in its turn with all the alluring colors of the long-lost. And so one swung backwards and forwards. Each world was best just as you left it for the other, as if to blow out these cloudy worlds and then to suck them in again were as functional as the rising and falling of the breast in sleep.",
        type: "Prose",
        notes: ["The sentence “It was in autumn” does not appear on 291 in this essay. It is first quoted in Walter Hooper’s bibliography in <i>C.S. Lewis: A Companion & Guide</i> (London: HarperCollins, 1996), 837, item D216, and again in his bibliography in <i>Remembering C.S. Lewis: Recollections of Those Who Knew Him,</i> 3rd edition, ed. James T. Como (San Francisco: Ignatius, 2005), 464, item D211."]
